+# Helpdesk API
+
+Rails API-only backend for a helpdesk SaaS. Businesses sign up, their owners verify
+email and sign in, and each business has its own users. The frontend is a separate
+app that talks to this API over JSON under `/api/v1`.
+
+## Setup
+
+Requires Ruby 4.0, PostgreSQL and the gems in the `Gemfile`.
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+export DATABASE_URL=postgresql:///helpdesk_development?host=/var/lib/postgresql
+bin/rails db:prepare                 # creates and migrates the development database
+RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:prepare  # the test database (a "_test" sibling of DATABASE_URL)
+bin/rails server                     # listens on PORT (default 3000)
+bin/rails test                       # the suite never touches the development database
+```
+
+Outgoing mail is written to `tmp/mails/` in development. Raw inbound mail is kept by Active Storage under `storage/` in development and production (see [Email support](#email-support)).
+
+### Environment variables
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `DATABASE_URL` | none (required) | PostgreSQL connection. |
+| `TEST_DATABASE_URL` | `DATABASE_URL` with `_development` replaced by `_test` | Test database. Tests never use the development database. |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` | Frontend base URL. Used for email links and as the only allowed CORS origin. |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | `support@example.test` | Sender address for all mail. |
+| `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` | `support.example.test` | Domain that receives support mail. Each business has `<subdomain>@<domain>`. |
+| `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD` | none | Password the mail server uses for the relay ingress. Without it, the relay answers `500` and accepts nothing. |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD` | `localhost`, `587`, — | Production mail delivery. |
+
+## Conventions
+
+- Every request body uses an envelope, e.g. `{"session": {...}}`. Bare JSON is rejected.
+- **Choosing a business:** send `X-Helpdesk-Account: <subdomain>`.
+  - Signed-in requests can omit it. The token already identifies the business. If it is sent and does not match, the response is 403.
+  - Sign-in, verification resend and password reset use it to find the user. Without it, the email must belong to exactly one business.
+- **Signed-in requests:** send `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
+- **Lists** can be paged by page number (`page`, `per_page`) or by cursor (`page[size]`, `page[after]`, `page[before]`). Both return the records under the plural resource name (`requests`, `tickets`, `comments`). See [Pagination](#pagination).
+- **Errors** share one shape: `{"error": {"code": "...", "message": "...", "errors": {...}}}`. `errors` is present for validation failures. Validation keys are prefixed `account.` or `admin.` on signup, and `comment.` on ticket replies.
+- Timestamps are ISO 8601 UTC.
+
+## Pagination
+
+Every list can be paged two ways: `requests`, `tickets`, the comments on a request or ticket, `users`, `staff`, `groups`, `group_memberships` and `invitations`. A request uses one way or the other.
+
+**Page numbers** (`?page=2&per_page=10`) are the original API, and their response does not change. It includes `count`, the total for the whole list, and `page`, `per_page`, `next_page` and `previous_page`. `next_page` and `previous_page` are `null` when there is no such page. Each page costs more the deeper it is, because the database walks past every skipped row, and `count` scans the whole list.
+
+**Cursors** are for browsing. Send `page[size]` (default 25, at most 100) and the response has `meta` and `links` instead of `count` and page numbers:
+
+```json
+{"tickets":[{"id":57}],"meta":{"has_more":true,"after_cursor":"eyJsaXN0Ijoi...","before_cursor":"eyJsaXN0Ijoi..."},"links":{"next":"https://.../api/v1/tickets?page%5Bafter%5D=eyJsaXN0Ijoi...&page%5Bsize%5D=25&status=open","prev":null}}
+```
+
+- `meta.after_cursor` is the cursor for the last record on the page, and `meta.before_cursor` is the cursor for the first. Both are `null` on an empty page.
+- Send `page[after]=<after_cursor>` for the records that come after the page, or `page[before]=<before_cursor>` for the records that come before it. Records are always listed in the usual order, so `page[before]` returns the nearest records before the page, still in order.
+- `links.next` and `links.prev` are ready-made URLs for those two pages. They keep the filters and the page size. A link is `null` when there is no page that way.
+- `meta.has_more` answers for the direction being read. Going forward (the first page, or `page[after]`) it says whether more records follow the page. With `page[before]` it says whether more records come before it.
+- Send at most one of `page[after]` and `page[before]`. With neither, you get the first page. `per_page` works as the page size when `page[size]` is not sent, and if both are sent they must agree.
+- Cursors are opaque, so do not build them. A value that is not a cursor from this list is `422` on `page.after` or `page.before`. A `page` that is both a number and a hash, as in `?page=2&page[size]=1`, is a `400`.
+- Filters work as before. A cursor is a place in the list, so the same cursor can be used with other filters: the next page is taken from that place in the newly filtered list.
+
+**What stays stable while others work:**
+
+- A cursor names a place in the list, not a row. It holds the sort values of the record it was taken from, so it keeps working if that record is edited or deleted.
+- New records do not repeat or shift the pages still to come. They show up when you start again from the first page.
+- Requests and tickets are listed by activity, most recent first, with `id` breaking ties. A request whose activity changes while you browse moves to the top, so a walk forward may not show it again. Start again from the first page to see where it is now.
+- Users, staff, groups, group memberships and invitations are listed by `id`, oldest first. Comments are listed oldest first as well.
+
+Cursor pages do not count the list, so the cost of a page does not grow with how deep it is.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+| Method and path | Auth | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /api/v1/registrations` | header required | Customer signs up for the business's portal, `{"user": {"name", "email", "password"}}`. Returns `201` with `user` (role `customer`). The user is unverified and gets a verification email. Then the usual verification, sign-in, profile and password-reset flows apply. An email with a pending invitation is refused with `422`, as if it were taken. |
+| `POST /api/v1/account/create` | none | Sign up a business and its admin. Returns `201` with `account` and `user`. The admin is unverified and receives a verification email. |
+| `GET /api/v1/portal` | header | Public `{"portal": {"name", "subdomain"}}`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/sessions` | header optional | Sign in with `{"session": {"email", "password"}}`. Returns `201` with `session.token`, `user` and `account`. Unverified users get `403 email_not_verified`. Suspended users get `403 user_suspended`. |
+| `DELETE /api/v1/session` | bearer | Sign out this session. `204`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/me` / `PATCH /api/v1/me` | bearer | Profile. `PATCH` takes `{"user": {"name": "..."}}`. |
+| `PUT /api/v1/me/password` | bearer | `{"password_change": {"current_password", "password"}}`. Signs out every session, including this one. |
+| `POST /api/v1/email_verifications` | header optional | `{"email_verification": {"email"}}`. Sends a new link and replaces the old one. Returns `202`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/email_verifications/confirm` | header optional | `{"email_verification": {"token"}}`. Marks the user verified. The token works once. |
+| `POST /api/v1/password_resets` | header optional | `{"password_reset": {"email"}}`. Sends a new link and replaces the old one. Returns `202` whether or not the email exists. |
+| `POST /api/v1/password_resets/confirm` | header optional | `{"password_reset": {"token", "password"}}`. Sets the password, signs out all sessions and verifies the email. The token works once. |
+| `GET /api/v1/account` | bearer | Full account, including `status` and `reactivation_deadline`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/account/cancel` | bearer, owner | Cancels the business and revokes all its sessions. |
+| `POST /api/v1/account/reactivate` | bearer, owner | Reactivates a cancelled business within 90 days. |
+| `GET /api/v1/requests` | bearer | The signed-in user's own requests, most recently active first, under `requests`. Filter with `?status=` set to `open`, `pending`, `solved` or `closed`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/requests` | bearer | Open a request: `{"request": {"subject", "comment": {"body"}}}`. Returns `201` with `request`, which starts `open`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/requests/:id` | bearer | One of your requests, under `request`. Another user's request returns `404`. |
+| `PATCH /api/v1/requests/:id` | bearer | Reply: `{"request": {"comment": {"body"}, "status": "solved"}}`. Either key is optional, but send at least one. See [Requests](#requests). Returns `request`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/requests/:id/comments` | bearer | Comments on your request, oldest first, under `comments`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/tickets` | bearer, staff | Every request in the business, most recently active first, under `tickets`. Each one names its `requester` as `{"id", "name", "email"}`, its assignee as `assignee_id` and `assignee` (`{"id", "name"}`, or `null`), and its `priority`. Filters, which combine: `status`, `priority`, `requester_id`, `group_id` (a group ID, or `unassigned`), `assignee_id` (a user ID, or `unassigned`) and `q`. See [Filtering](#filtering). |
+| `GET /api/v1/tickets/:id` | bearer, staff | One request, under `ticket`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/tickets/:id/merge` | bearer, staff | Merge this duplicate into another request of the same customer: `{"merge": {"target_id": 57, "source_comment": {"body", "public"}, "target_comment": {"body", "public"}}}`. Closes this request and returns `source` and `target`. See [Merging](#merging). |
+| `PATCH /api/v1/tickets/:id` | bearer, staff | Staff reply: `{"ticket": {"comment": {"body", "public"}, "status": "pending", "assignee_id": 12, "group_id": 20, "priority": "high"}}`. Every key is optional, but send at least one. `assignee_id` takes a user ID, or `null` to unassign. `group_id` takes a group ID, or `null` to remove the request from its group. See [Groups](#groups). `priority` takes `normal` or `high`. `comment.public` defaults to `true`. Send `false` for a private note. Returns `ticket`. See [Requests](#requests). |
+| `GET /api/v1/tickets/:id/comments` | bearer, staff | Every comment on the request, public and private, oldest first, under `comments`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/staff` | bearer, staff | Active staff (admins and agents who are not suspended), by ID, under `staff` as `{"id", "name", "role"}`. For assignment controls. |
+| `POST /api/v1/invitations` | bearer, admin | Invite someone: `{"invitation": {"email", "role"}}`, where `role` is `admin` or `agent`. Returns `201` with `invitation` and emails the link. See [Invitations](#invitations). |
+| `GET /api/v1/invitations` | bearer, admin | Invitations by ID, oldest first, under `invitations`. Filter with `?status=` set to `pending`, `accepted`, `revoked` or `expired`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/invitations/:id` | bearer, admin | One invitation, under `invitation`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/invitations/:id/resend` | bearer, admin | Send a new link. The old one stops working. Works on pending and expired invitations. Returns `invitation`. |
+| `DELETE /api/v1/invitations/:id` | bearer, admin | Revoke the invitation. Its link stops working. The record is kept, as `revoked`. Returns `invitation`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/invitation_previews` | none | Before accepting: `{"invitation_preview": {"token"}}`. Returns `invitation_preview` with `email`, `role`, `business_name`, `subdomain` and `expires_at`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/invitation_acceptances` | none | Accept: `{"invitation_acceptance": {"token", "name", "password"}}`. Creates a verified user. Returns `201` with `user` and `account`. Sign in as usual. |
+| `GET /api/v1/users` | bearer, admin | Every person in the business, by ID, under `users`. Filter with `?role=` set to `admin`, `agent` or `customer`, and with `?suspended=` set to `true` or `false`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/users/:id` | bearer, admin | One person, under `user`. |
+| `PATCH /api/v1/users/:id` | bearer, admin | Suspend or reinstate: `{"user": {"suspended": true}}`. Returns `user`. Other fields are ignored. See [Suspension](#suspension). |
+| `GET /api/v1/groups` | bearer, staff | Every group in the business, by ID, under `groups`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/groups` | bearer, admin | Create a group: `{"group": {"name": "Billing"}}`. Returns `201` with `group`. See [Groups](#groups). |
+| `GET /api/v1/groups/:id` | bearer, staff | One group, under `group`. |
+| `PATCH /api/v1/groups/:id` | bearer, admin | Rename: `{"group": {"name": "Accounts"}}`. Returns `group`. |
+| `DELETE /api/v1/groups/:id` | bearer, admin | Delete the group and its memberships. Its requests stay, ungrouped and without an assignee. `204`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/group_memberships` | bearer, staff | Memberships by ID, under `group_memberships`. Filter with `?group_id=` and `?user_id=`. |
+| `POST /api/v1/group_memberships` | bearer, admin | Add a staff member to a group: `{"group_membership": {"group_id": 20, "user_id": 12, "default": true}}`. `default` is optional. Returns `201` with `group_membership`. |
+| `GET /api/v1/group_memberships/:id` | bearer, staff | One membership, under `group_membership`. |
+| `PUT /api/v1/group_memberships/:id/make_default` | bearer, admin | Make this the person's default group. Returns `group_membership`. |
+| `DELETE /api/v1/group_memberships/:id` | bearer, admin | Remove the person from the group. `204`. |
+
+Emailed links:
+
+- `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/verify-email?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`
+- `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/reset-password?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`
+- `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/accept-invitation?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`
+
+Token lifetimes are 3 days for verification, 2 hours for password reset and 7 days for invitations. Sessions last 30 days.
+
+## Accounts and roles
+
+- The signing-up user is an `admin` and acts as the business owner. Only admins can cancel or reactivate.
+- An `agent` is staff, like an admin. Admins create agents and other admins with [invitations](#invitations).
+- Only admins manage people, invitations, groups and group memberships. Agents and admins both work the business's requests. Admin-only routes return `403 admin_only` to everyone else.
+- A `customer` registers through the portal (`POST /registrations`). Customers can only reach their own requests. Staff-only routes return `403 staff_only` to them.
+- Email addresses are unique within a business, so one email can belong to several businesses.
+- Subdomains are unique across all businesses and must not be reserved (`www`, `api`, `app`, `admin`, ...).
+
+### Cancellation
+
+- `POST /account/cancel` sets `cancelled_at` and revokes every session in the business.
+- While cancelled, only the owner can sign in. Ordinary access returns `403 account_cancelled`. The owner can still view the account, edit their profile, change their password, sign out, and reactivate.
+- `POST /account/reactivate` works until 90 days after cancellation.
+- Data is kept until the reactivation window closes.
+
+### Permanent deletion
+
+```sh
+bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+This deletes every account cancelled more than 90 days ago, along with its users, sessions, tokens, groups, memberships, requests and comments. Run it on a schedule, for example daily from cron. It is safe to run repeatedly.
+
+## Requests
+
+A request is one record with a subject, a status and a conversation. Customers see it at `/requests`, and staff at `/tickets`. Both views are the same records.
+
+- A new request is `open`. Opening one needs a `subject` and a first `comment.body`. Both are saved, or neither is.
+- Statuses are `open`, `pending`, `solved` and `closed`. Only a merge sets `closed`. See [Merging](#merging).
+- **Customer replies** can carry a comment, a status, or both. A status can only be `solved`, so `pending` or `open` is refused with `422`. A comment reopens a `pending` or `solved` request, unless the same update marks it `solved`.
+- **Staff replies** can carry a comment, a status, an assignee, or a combination. Any of `open`, `pending` or `solved` is allowed, but not `closed`. A reply without a status leaves the status alone, so a staff reply never reopens a request by itself.
+- A reply with a comment and a status is applied as one update. If either part is invalid, nothing is saved.
+- **Private notes**: staff can send `comment: {body, public: false}`. Only staff see them. A customer's comment is always public, so `public` is ignored for them. Anything other than `true` or `false` for `public` is refused with `422`, so a typo cannot post a note publicly. Customers see only public comments on their own requests. Staff see every comment.
+- Comment bodies are limited to 10,000 characters. Subjects are limited to 255.
+- Replies on requests from the portal send no email. A public staff reply on a request that came in by email is emailed to the requester. See [Email support](#email-support).
+- Activity moves a request up the lists: opening, replying with a public comment, or changing its status. Comments are listed oldest first.
+- **Internal activity leaves the customer's view unchanged.** A private note, an assignment or a priority change sent on its own does not change the request's `status` or `updated_at`, so the request keeps its place in the lists and the customer sees nothing new. A status sent with them still applies, as it always does.
+- **Assignment**: `assignee_id` names a staff member who is active (an admin or agent, not suspended) in the same business, and who belongs to the request's group when it has one. Anything else is refused with `422` on `assignee_id`, and nothing in that update is saved. See [Groups](#groups) for what else changes with the group. Customers cannot assign requests.
+- **Priority** is `normal` or `high`, and a new request is `normal`. Staff set it with `priority` on a reply, alone or with the rest. `null` and anything else are refused with `422` on `priority`, and nothing in that update is saved. Priority is internal: it appears in staff `ticket` responses only, never in a customer's `request`. A customer's `priority` is ignored, so a reply with only a `priority` is a bad request, as with `assignee_id`.
+
+### Merging
+
+A customer often opens several requests about the same problem. Staff merge a duplicate into the request that survives, with `POST /tickets/:id/merge`. The request in the path is the duplicate, and `target_id` names the survivor:
+
+```json
+{"merge": {"target_id": 57, "source_comment": {"body": "Continuing in ticket 57.", "public": true}, "target_comment": {"body": "Duplicate checked.", "public": false}}}
+```
+
+- **Closing.** The duplicate's status becomes `closed`, and its `merged_into_id` is the survivor's ID. It stays readable. Everything else on it is kept, including its comments. Customers see the same `merged_into_id` on their requests, so both can follow the link. Unmerged requests show `merged_into_id: null`.
+- **Nothing else moves.** The duplicate's comments stay where they are. The survivor keeps its status, assignee, group, priority, requester and reply address.
+- **Notes.** Each side gets one note from the staff member. A note is private unless its `public` is `true`. A missing `body` takes the default text: `Merged into #57. The conversation continues there.` on the duplicate, and `Merged in from #42.` on the survivor. Notes never send email, even when public.
+- **Response.** `200` with `source` and `target`, both in the staff `ticket` shape.
+- **Targets.** The survivor must be another request in the business, belong to the same customer, and not be `closed`. Anything else is `422` on `target_id`, and nothing is saved.
+- **Notes that are refused.** A blank or non-text `body`, a `body` over 10,000 characters, a `public` that is not `true` or `false`, or a note that is not an object is `422`, naming the field, such as `source_comment.body`. Nothing is merged.
+- **Missing values.** Without `merge` or without `target_id` the request is a bad request (`400`). Customers get `403 staff_only`.
+- **Closed requests are read-only.** Any staff or customer reply, status, assignee, group or priority change on a closed request is refused with `422 ticket_closed`. A closed request cannot be merged again, and it cannot take a merge. Mail to its private reply address is refused, with no notice, like other refused mail.
+- **Cleanup still applies.** Suspending an assignee, removing someone from a group and deleting a group still clear them from closed requests, as they do from open ones. Those clears do not move the request's timestamps.
+- **Links are kept.** When the survivor is later merged into another request, the duplicates that already point at it keep pointing at it. Only the request merged now gets a new pointer, so the history is not rewritten.
+- **Nothing reopens a closed request.** Its history stays as it was.
+
+### Filtering
+
+- `GET /tickets` filters by `status` (`open`, `pending`, `solved` or `closed`), `priority`, `requester_id`, `assignee_id` and `q`. The filters combine, so a request must match every one that is sent, for example `GET /tickets?status=open&assignee_id=unassigned&priority=high&q=receipt`.
+- `requester_id` and `assignee_id` take a user ID. `assignee_id=unassigned` means nobody is assigned. An unknown user ID matches nothing.
+- `group_id` takes a group ID. `group_id=unassigned` means the request is in no group. An unknown group ID matches nothing.
+- `q` is a case-insensitive search through subjects and public comments. Private notes are not searched. The text is literal: `%`, `_` and `\` match only themselves. A blank `q` is no search.
+- `GET /requests` filters a customer's own requests by `status` only. Other filters, `priority` included, are for staff and are ignored there.
+- A blank filter value means no filter. A value that is not allowed is refused with `422`, naming the parameter, as in `{"error": {"code": "validation_failed", ..."errors": {"status": ["must be one of open, pending, solved"]}}}`.
+
+## Groups
+
+Groups are the teams that work requests, such as Billing or Technical Support. Staff can belong to several groups. A group organizes the work and never hides a request: every staff member sees every request, whatever their groups.
+
+- **Names** are required, at most 120 characters, and unique within the business, ignoring case. Surrounding spaces are removed.
+- **Memberships** join one staff member, an admin or agent, to one group, once. Customers cannot be members. Suspended staff can still be given memberships, and they are kept for when the person returns.
+- **Default group.** A staff member with memberships has exactly one default. The first membership is always the default, whatever `default` says. A membership with `"default": true` takes the default from the previous one, and `PUT .../make_default` does the same. Removing the default makes the oldest remaining membership the default. Removing the last membership leaves the person with no default.
+- **Requests** have an optional group. Staff `ticket` responses show it as `group_id` and `group` (`{"id", "name"}`, or `null`). Customers never see it.
+- **An assignee must belong to the request's group.** A request with no group can have any active staff member as assignee. Naming an assignee who is not in the group is refused with `422`, and nothing in that update is saved.
+- **Assigning someone to a request with no group** puts it in their default group, if they have one. Otherwise it stays ungrouped, with the assignee. A group named in the same update is used as given, not replaced by the default.
+- **Moving a request to another group** clears an assignee who is not in the new group. This is silent, unless the same update names that assignee, which is refused as above.
+- **Clearing the assignee** leaves the group in place.
+- **Clearing the group** (`"group_id": null`) also clears the assignee, unless the same update names one.
+- **Removing someone from a group** clears them as assignee on that group's requests. Those requests keep their group, and the person keeps their other memberships.
+- **Deleting a group** removes its memberships, and clears the group and the assignee on its requests. Each affected person keeps their other memberships, and gets a new default if this was theirs.
+- **Suspension** clears a suspended person's assignments and keeps their memberships. See [Suspension](#suspension).
+- A group change is internal activity, like an assignment. It does not move the request up the lists, and the customer sees nothing new.
+
+## Invitations
+
+Admins add people by inviting them. Only `admin` and `agent` can be invited. Customers register through the portal instead.
+
+- `POST /invitations` emails a link to `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/accept-invitation?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`. The API never returns the token, and stores only its digest.
+- An email can have **one pending invitation per business**, whatever the role. A second one is refused with `422`. Resend the first one instead. An email that already belongs to someone in the business cannot be invited either.
+- Invitations are **pending**, **accepted**, **revoked** or **expired**. Expiry comes from the clock, so an expired invitation needs no job. An expired invitation no longer blocks a fresh one for the same email.
+- Accepted and revoked invitations can no longer be resent or revoked, with `422 invitation_closed`. Resending a pending or expired invitation issues a new token and restarts the 7-day clock.
+- Accepting creates the user with the invited email and role, already verified. The new person signs in straight away, with no further email.
+- Customer registration refuses an email that has a pending invitation, reporting it as taken. The portal does not reveal who has been invited.
+- Accepting refuses a business that has been cancelled, with `403 account_cancelled`.
+
+## Suspension
+
+- An admin suspends or reinstates anyone in the business with `PATCH /users/:id`.
+- Suspending signs the person out of every session, clears the tickets they were assigned, and stops them signing in (`403 user_suspended`). Reinstating does not bring back their old sessions, so they sign in again.
+- The last active admin cannot be suspended, not even by themselves. This is checked under the business lock, so two admins cannot suspend each other at the same moment.
+- Suspended people are not offered as assignees, and are left out of `GET /staff`. Their group memberships are kept, so they are back in their groups when reinstated.
+
+## Concurrency
+
+Creating and resending invitations, accepting them, customer registration, suspension, any change of assignee or group, and every group change (creating, renaming and deleting groups, and adding, removing and choosing default memberships) each run under the business's row lock. That keeps the rules above (one pending invitation per email, no registration over an invitation, at least one active admin, no suspended assignee, one default group per person, and no assignee outside the request's group) true under concurrent requests.
+
+## Email support
+
+Customers can email a business's support address and reply from their mail client. Action Mailbox receives the mail.
+
+- **Addresses.** Each business has `<subdomain>@<HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN>`, for example `acme@support.example.test`. Each request also has a private reply address, `<subdomain>+<key>@<domain>`. Staff replies carry it as `Reply-To`.
+- **Ingress.** The mail server pipes each message to `POST /rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails` as `message/rfc822`, using HTTP Basic auth with user `actionmailbox` and the password in `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD`. A wrong password gets `401`, and a body that is not `message/rfc822` gets `415`. Mail is processed in the request that delivers it in test, so no worker is needed there.
+- **Finding the business.** Every recipient at the support domain counts, from To, Cc and Bcc, and from forwarding headers such as `X-Original-To`. The mail is for the business its recipients name. Mail addressed to two or more businesses is ignored, and so is mail that names no business. A cancelled business refuses mail.
+- **New request.** Mail to a business's plain address opens a request. The subject is the request's subject (`(no subject)` when there is none, and long subjects are shortened to 255 characters). The first public comment is the message's plain text. An HTML-only message is converted to text, and attachments are not part of it. Text over 10,000 characters is shortened, with a note saying so.
+- **Customers.** An existing user with the sender's address in the business is reused, whatever their role. Suspended people are refused. A new sender gets an unverified customer account with a random password, and a verification email. Receiving mail never verifies anyone. The new customer sets a password through the password reset flow, which also verifies the email.
+- **Replies.** Mail to a request's private reply address adds a public comment, from the requester only. Mail from anyone else is refused. A reply reopens a `pending` or `solved` request, and an `open` one stays open. A `closed` request, which was merged into another, refuses replies, and its reply address stops taking mail. A reply that also names the business's plain address is still a reply, not a new request.
+- **Staff replies.** A public staff reply on a request that came in by email is emailed to the requester from the business's support address, with the private reply address as `Reply-To`. Replies are threaded under the requester's messages with `In-Reply-To` and `References`. Private notes, status changes and assignments send no email.
+- **Duplicates and retries.** A message whose Message-ID was already accepted is dropped when it arrives, however many times it is delivered, so it is not recorded as a second delivery. Only accepted messages record their Message-ID, on the comment they create. A refused message therefore leaves nothing behind, and a later delivery of the same message is processed normally.
+- **Automated and list mail is ignored.** That covers `Auto-Submitted` other than `no`, `Precedence` of `bulk`, `list`, `junk` or `auto_reply`, `List-Id`, `List-Unsubscribe`, auto-responder headers, bounce reports and mail from `mailer-daemon`, `postmaster`, `noreply` and similar senders. Mail sent from a support address is ignored too, so messages cannot loop.
+- **Refused mail gets no notice.** It is recorded as `bounced` in Action Mailbox's table, which doubles as a log. Nothing is sent back to the sender.
+- **Limits.** The sender is identified by the `From` header, which is not verified. Anyone who knows a private reply address and can forge the requester's `From` can add to that request. Mail with no text (for example an attachment alone) is refused, and attachments are not kept on the request.
+
+## Layout
+
+- `app/models/account.rb`: business, cancellation, reactivation window, purge.
+- `app/models/user.rb`: users, roles, password rules.
+- `app/models/session.rb`: bearer sessions (only token digests are stored).
+- `app/models/group.rb`, `app/models/group_membership.rb`: groups, memberships, default groups, and the cleanup when either changes.
+- `app/models/ticket.rb`: requests, their status rules, group, assignment and merging. `app/models/comment.rb`: the conversation, with private notes.
+- `app/models/invitation.rb`: invitations, their statuses, acceptance and the one-pending-per-email rule.
+- `app/models/concerns/single_use_token.rb`: replaceable, single-use tokens for verification and reset.
+- `app/controllers/concerns/`: error format, business lookup from header, bearer auth and cancelled-account rules, pagination, list filters, reply parsing, and strict true/false parsing.
+- `lib/keyset_cursor.rb`: cursors for the cursor pages. It reads each list's sort from its query and seeks to the position the cursor names.
+- `app/controllers/api/v1/`: endpoints.
+- `app/mailers/user_mailer.rb`: verification, reset and invitation emails. `app/mailers/ticket_mailer.rb`: staff replies to requesters on email requests.
+- `app/mailboxes/`: `ApplicationMailbox` routes all inbound mail to `SupportMailbox`, which opens or continues requests. `SupportMail` reads one message (recipients, sender, subject, text, automated-mail checks).
+- `config/storage.yml`: Active Storage services for the raw inbound mail.
+- `config/initializers/helpdesk.rb`: settings from the environment. `config/initializers/cors.rb`: frontend CORS.
